# `_` in each of the three name positions a declaration has. The lexer gives
# it back as the wildcard rather than as a word, so `type`, `fn` and the
# top-level line rule each turn it away with their own message.
#
# leading_underscore covers the other half: any word longer than one character
# that starts with `_` is refused at lex time. Between the two, no declared
# name in a kanso program can begin with an underscore — which is what let the
# unused-private check go on being compiled after the convention it was written
# for was retired.
type _
  n

fn _ x
  x

_ = 1

pub play = print "x"
